# ── Models (large GGUF weights — never commit) ──────────────
*.gguf
models/mistral-7b-instruct-v0.2.Q4_K_M.gguf
archiso/airootfs/var/lib/synapd/models/synapse-7b-q4_k_m.gguf
archiso/airootfs/var/lib/synapd/models/synapse.gguf
# Written by archiso/build.sh next to the weights above, with a build timestamp
# in it, so it reappears modified after every ISO build. Nothing reads it — only
# build.sh mentions it — and build.sh mkdir -p's the directory (line 244)
# regardless of --no-model, as do syn-firstboot and syn-model before they
# download, so untracking it costs neither the file nor the directory.
archiso/airootfs/var/lib/synapd/models/manifest.txt

# ── Vendored / prebuilt llama.cpp (build it locally; see README) ─
llama.cpp/
# llama-staging* covers the per-backend trees (llama-staging-cpu,
# llama-staging-cuda), the bare `llama-staging` symlink that points at the last
# one built, and the symlink makepkg leaves in synapd/src/.
llama-staging*
archiso/build/

# ── makepkg build droppings ─────────────────────────────────
# package staging dirs
pkg/
*/pkg/
# extracted source tree (makepkg unpacks the tarball into src/)
src/*-0.1.0/
*/src/*-0.1.0/
# Generated source tarballs. These are build artifacts, not sources — the
# working tree is the source. A fresh clone regenerates them with either
# ./build-all.sh (which does it per component) or, for synui and synguard,
# <pkg>/mktarball.sh. Do NOT commit them.
#
# Both PKGBUILDs refuse to build a tarball older than the tree, so a stale one
# can no longer be packaged silently — which it once was, shipping a synguard
# binary without the fix that had just been built into it.
*-0.1.0.tar.gz
# makepkg srcdir for the script-only packages (canonical source is the
# top-level <pkg>/<name>.sh; everything makepkg drops into src/ is generated)
syn/src/
syn-model/src/
syn-firstboot/src/
syn-update/src/
syn-install/src/
syn-arsenal/src/
# samsung-m2020 now DOWNLOADS Samsung's ULD tarball instead of vendoring it, so
# src/ holds the extracted proprietary driver — every vendor PPD and binary in
# the release, not just the four files this packages. Committing it is the exact
# thing the switch away from vendoring was for, and `git add -A` in this
# directory WILL sweep it in: the extraction dir is `uld/`, so the generic
# src/*-0.1.0/ rule above does not match it.
samsung-m2020/src/
samsung-m2020/uld_*.tar.gz
# makepkg srcdir + downloaded upstream tarball for the scenefx packages. The
# canonical source is upstream's tagged release (pinned in each PKGBUILD) plus
# the six ported wlroots scene TUs sitting next to it; everything under src/ is
# a throwaway extraction. Committing it would let a stale upstream tree be
# rebuilt silently — the same trap synui/mktarball.sh guards against.
scenefx/src/
scenefx/scenefx-*.tar.gz
scenefx0.5/src/
scenefx0.5/scenefx-*.tar.gz
# makepkg srcdir for the web-app packages — canonical source is each app's own
# git repo, pinned by commit in its PKGBUILD; src/ is a throwaway clone
nexus-chat/src/
tepris/src/
# Same shape, but a THIRD-PARTY upstream (areofyl/fetch): the canonical source
# is that repo at the pinned _commit, plus the two patches next to the PKGBUILD,
# which ARE tracked — they are the whole of what SynapseOS changed.
fetch/src/
# makepkg's cached bare clone of each app's upstream repo — SRCDEST is the
# PKGBUILD dir, so it lands as <pkg>/<pkg>/. The pinned _commit in the PKGBUILD
# is the real reference; this is just a download cache.
nexus-chat/nexus-chat/
tepris/tepris/
fetch/fetch/
# vendored cdnjs libs makepkg downloads next to nexus-chat/PKGBUILD (their
# sha256s are pinned there, so they're reproducible, not source)
nexus-chat/*.min.js
# built packages
*.pkg.tar.zst
*.pkg.tar.xz
# python bytecode (vibe is vendored in-tree; its .pyc are build/runtime cruft)
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# ── meson / ninja build dirs ────────────────────────────────
build/
builddir/
builddir-asan/
_ci/
_asan/

